VALUES ('33e0acfc-a5b6-464a-9174-114fd493b942', 'secplus-practice-6', 'CompTIA Security+ (SY0-701) Practice Paper 6', 'comptia-sec-plus', ARRAY['Gap Analysis', 'Rootkit', 'DNS Poisoning', 'BYOD', 'Secure Boot', 'SAST/DAST', 'NAC', 'Threat Modeling', 'Data Sovereignty', 'SLA vs MOU']::TEXT[], 20, 'standard', true, 6, 30);

-- ── Domain: General Security Concepts (12% — 2 questions) ──

VALUES ('4dd35f8a-98c9-47b0-b46c-4dfea9001f24', '33e0acfc-a5b6-464a-9174-114fd493b942', 1, 'A security team wants to formally understand exactly how far their organization''s CURRENT security posture (existing controls, documented processes, actual practices) falls short of a DESIRED target state (a chosen framework''s requirements, an industry benchmark, or leadership''s stated risk-tolerance goals), specifically to build a prioritized roadmap of what needs to change. What is this formal comparison process called?');
VALUES ('4dd35f8a-98c9-47b0-b46c-4dfea9001f24', '5719b83c-4f99-4f04-be3d-fec7f3a9ee3f', 'A gap analysis is specifically the structured process of comparing "where we are" against "where we want or need to be" — often measured against a chosen security framework (connecting to the framework-vs-regulation concept covered in a prior paper), a specific compliance requirement, or an internally-defined target risk posture — and its defining output is a concrete, prioritized LIST OF GAPS (not a pass/fail verdict, and not a general audit report), which security leadership then uses to build an actual remediation roadmap with sequenced priorities. This is a genuinely foundational planning tool distinct from a compliance audit (covered in a prior paper, which verifies adherence to a SPECIFIC standard and typically produces a formal attestation or finding, rather than a broader current-vs-desired-state roadmap input) and from a risk register (covered in a prior paper, which tracks individual identified RISKS rather than a structured current-versus-target STATE comparison) — a tabletop exercise (covered in a prior paper, a discussion-based incident-response rehearsal) and a honeytoken (a decoy data tripwire) are both genuinely unrelated concepts to this specific planning process.');

VALUES ('34fe1746-f89b-49cc-b5d7-f5b9b635bb29', '33e0acfc-a5b6-464a-9174-114fd493b942', 2, 'A company''s governance documentation includes: a high-level statement that "all remote access must be encrypted" (set by leadership), a specific document stating "remote access must use TLS 1.2 or higher" (a measurable, mandatory requirement), a step-by-step document explaining exactly how to configure a specific VPN client (a repeatable how-to), and a document recommending (but not requiring) a specific password manager. What are these four distinct types of governance documentation called, from most authoritative/general to most optional?');
VALUES ('34fe1746-f89b-49cc-b5d7-f5b9b635bb29', 'b32b9a56-e371-4f80-a4f6-f49e43d837bd', 'This policy/standard/procedure/guideline hierarchy is a foundational governance documentation structure, genuinely distinct from any single named document like an AUP (covered in a prior paper, itself an example of a specific POLICY) — a policy sets the high-level, mandatory INTENT ("what" and "why," set by leadership, generally technology-agnostic and slow to change); a standard translates that intent into a specific, measurable, still-MANDATORY requirement (a specific TLS version, a specific password length); a procedure is the detailed, repeatable "how" for accomplishing a specific task in service of a standard or policy; and a guideline is explicitly NOT mandatory — a recommended best practice organizations may choose to follow but aren''t compliance-bound to. The genuinely important, testable distinction is the DECREASING mandatory authority moving from policy through standard through procedure to guideline, and the increasing technical SPECIFICITY moving the same direction — these four terms are precise, standard governance vocabulary, not interchangeable synonyms for the same kind of document, and this classification structure is distinct from (though it can certainly document) either a risk register (covered in a prior paper, which tracks individual risks) or a gap analysis (covered earlier this paper, a current-vs-target-state comparison process).');

-- ── Domain: Threats, Vulnerabilities & Mitigations (22% — 4 questions) ──

VALUES ('7fd196d3-ed01-49c6-85bf-65d50bc5a410', '33e0acfc-a5b6-464a-9174-114fd493b942', 3, 'Malware is discovered on a server that has modified the operating system''s own kernel-level components specifically to hide its own running processes, files, and network connections from standard system tools and even from many security products — the compromised system''s own OS reports nothing unusual is running. What type of malware is specifically designed for this kind of deep, OS-level concealment?');
VALUES ('7fd196d3-ed01-49c6-85bf-65d50bc5a410', '613f98a4-c5db-4edb-820d-247db138bf7d', 'A rootkit is specifically defined by its concealment MECHANISM — rather than just being malicious code that runs alongside normal processes, a rootkit specifically tampers with the OS itself (frequently at the kernel/driver level, the most privileged execution layer) so that standard system-reporting tools (process lists, file listings, network connection tables) are themselves manipulated to simply not show the malware''s presence, since the compromised OS is the very thing being asked to report on itself. This genuinely distinct mechanism is exactly why rootkit detection typically requires specialized tools that don''t rely on the potentially-compromised OS''s own reporting (e.g., booting from external trusted media to inspect the system, or behavioral/memory-level analysis that doesn''t depend on the OS''s self-report) — a meaningfully harder detection problem than malware that doesn''t attempt this kind of deep concealment, connecting to why EDR (covered in a prior paper) specifically watches BEHAVIOR at a level closer to the hardware/hypervisor rather than trusting OS-level self-reporting alone. Ransomware (covered in a prior paper) is defined by its ENCRYPT-and-extort BEHAVIOR, not by an OS-concealment mechanism, and a watering hole attack (covered in a prior paper) is an unrelated delivery/compromise TECHNIQUE — this deep OS-level concealment mechanism is a real, well-documented, and historically significant malware category, not a technical impossibility.');

VALUES ('83b3c4c6-cfac-4997-96ed-416ca4e0e2e5', '33e0acfc-a5b6-464a-9174-114fd493b942', 4, 'A disgruntled systems administrator, before being terminated, embeds hidden code into a critical production application that lies dormant and causes no harm at all — UNTIL a specific future date arrives (or a specific triggering condition occurs, like the administrator''s own account being deleted from the payroll system), at which point it deletes critical data. What type of malicious code is this specifically called?');
VALUES ('83b3c4c6-cfac-4997-96ed-416ca4e0e2e5', 'f825b7f5-c708-468d-866e-955b9604ac54', 'A logic bomb is specifically defined by its TIMING/TRIGGER mechanism rather than its payload or delivery method — deliberately remaining completely inert and undetectable-by-behavior until a specific, predefined condition is satisfied (a calendar date, a specific event occurring, or notably, a condition NOT occurring, like a specific employee account remaining active — a classic, real, and well-documented insider-threat pattern connecting directly to the insider threat concept covered in a prior paper, since logic bombs are frequently planted by a disgruntled insider with legitimate access specifically BEFORE their access is revoked). This delayed-activation characteristic is exactly what makes logic bombs genuinely hard to detect through behavioral monitoring during the dormant period, since there''s no anomalous activity to observe until the trigger condition actually fires — a meaningfully different threat profile from malware that behaves suspiciously from the moment of initial compromise. A rootkit (covered earlier this paper) is defined by its OS-level CONCEALMENT mechanism, not by delayed, condition-based triggering — a genuinely different, though sometimes combined, characteristic, and steganography (covered in a prior paper) is a data-hiding technique entirely unrelated to code execution timing.');

VALUES ('3ab85a8e-389d-42b9-ad4c-bb30d4489050', '33e0acfc-a5b6-464a-9174-114fd493b942', 5, 'An attacker compromises a DNS resolver (or poisons its cache) so that when a victim''s browser requests the IP address for their bank''s legitimate domain name, the resolver returns the IP address of the attacker''s own fake, look-alike website instead — even though the victim typed the correct, legitimate domain name themselves. What is this attack called?');
VALUES ('3ab85a8e-389d-42b9-ad4c-bb30d4489050', '91198453-5ad1-40a9-950a-60b4606e1ad3', 'DNS poisoning specifically targets the DOMAIN-NAME-TO-IP-ADDRESS resolution step itself, rather than the victim''s typing/input (unlike typosquatting, a different, related attack relying on a victim mistyping a domain) or the network path between victim and server (unlike an evil twin or general on-path position, covered in a prior paper) — by corrupting a DNS resolver''s cache (or otherwise manipulating the DNS resolution chain) with a false record, the attacker ensures that even a victim who correctly types the LEGITIMATE domain name gets silently redirected to the attacker''s IP address, with the browser''s address bar still showing the correct, legitimate domain name throughout, since DNS is a purely behind-the-scenes lookup step invisible to normal users — a genuinely dangerous characteristic making this attack hard for an ordinary victim to notice at all. This connects directly to why HTTPS/TLS certificate validation (covered in a prior paper, via PKI concepts) provides a real, independent layer of protection even against a successful DNS poisoning attack, since a certificate issued to the legitimate domain generally won''t validate correctly against the attacker''s server. A logic bomb (covered earlier this paper) and credential stuffing (covered in a prior paper) are both genuinely unrelated mechanisms to DNS-level manipulation — this is a real, well-documented, and historically significant attack technique, not an impossibility.');

VALUES ('90c23db8-2260-4d8f-8229-a82e4293e682', '33e0acfc-a5b6-464a-9174-114fd493b942', 6, 'An attacker deliberately leaves several USB flash drives, each labeled "Executive Compensation 2026 — Confidential," scattered in a company''s parking lot and lobby, hoping a curious employee will pick one up, plug it into a work computer out of curiosity, and unknowingly execute malware embedded on the drive. What social engineering technique specifically describes exploiting a target''s curiosity with a planted physical or digital lure like this?');
VALUES ('90c23db8-2260-4d8f-8229-a82e4293e682', 'f9f0611e-f283-4ee0-a53a-c423bdf1b814', 'Baiting is specifically defined by relying on a PASSIVE lure (the enticing bait itself doing the work) rather than an active, interactive manipulation — this is a genuinely distinct mechanism from pretexting (covered in a prior paper, which specifically requires constructing and actively delivering a fabricated, believable SCENARIO through direct interaction, like a phone call to a help desk) or BEC/phishing (covered in prior papers, which require crafting and sending a targeted, interactive message), since baiting simply requires planting the enticing lure somewhere the target will discover it on their own, relying entirely on the target''s own curiosity or greed to complete the compromise with no further attacker interaction needed at all. A labeled, intriguing USB drive left in a plausible location (a parking lot, a lobby) is a classic, real, and well-documented baiting example specifically because curiosity is a genuinely reliable human trigger — and this is emphatically a REAL, demonstrated attack vector (numerous documented penetration-testing engagements and real incidents have successfully used exactly this technique), not an unrealistic scenario, which is exactly why security awareness training (covered in a prior paper) specifically includes "never plug in an unknown USB device" as standard guidance.');

-- ── Domain: Security Architecture (18% — 4 questions) ──

VALUES ('ef8d0677-b8bd-4326-be87-ba2826e7a24b', '33e0acfc-a5b6-464a-9174-114fd493b942', 7, 'A company is deciding how employees will use mobile devices for work: option A lets employees use their own personally-owned phones with a company-managed security profile installed; option B has the company purchase and fully own the devices, with employees choosing from an approved model list and using it for both work and limited personal use. What are these two mobile device deployment models called, and what is the key security trade-off between them?');
VALUES ('ef8d0677-b8bd-4326-be87-ba2826e7a24b', 'f012062e-9278-49a3-b377-8dcd7f19af65', 'BYOD and COPE sit at genuinely different points on the ownership/control spectrum — BYOD''s core defining characteristic is that the DEVICE ITSELF remains the employee''s personal property, which fundamentally limits how much security control an organization can reasonably impose (aggressive remote-wipe capability, for instance, becomes a real point of employee privacy concern and potential legal complication on a personally-owned device mixing personal and work data), even though a company-managed security PROFILE or container can still be installed to protect the work-related portion specifically. COPE flips this: the organization owns the device outright, which allows a meaningfully stronger, more consistent security posture to be enforced (full device management, stricter policy enforcement) since there''s no competing personal-property/privacy tension to navigate, at the real cost of higher procurement/management expense and less device choice for employees compared to BYOD''s "use whatever you already have" flexibility. (A third model, CYOD — Choose Your Own Device, letting employees pick from an approved list of company-owned devices — sits conceptually between these two, though not directly asked about here.) This is a real, standard, and directly testable distinction in mobile device security strategy, not an arbitrary or meaningless labeling exercise.');

VALUES ('adcd0820-1f13-4bdd-be75-aa3ed60bed7b', '33e0acfc-a5b6-464a-9174-114fd493b942', 8, 'A network security audit discovers that several internal systems still use Telnet for remote administration (transmitting commands and credentials in plaintext) and SNMPv1 for device monitoring (also lacking encryption and using a weak, easily-guessed default community string). What is the standard remediation approach, and what secure protocols should replace these legacy ones?');
VALUES ('adcd0820-1f13-4bdd-be75-aa3ed60bed7b', 'dc0a3702-0e8c-4f6d-89d4-130ffe51870d', 'Deliberately selecting SECURE protocol variants over their legacy, insecure counterparts is a foundational, standard security architecture practice — Telnet transmits every character of a session, including login credentials, in completely readable plaintext across the network, making it trivially interceptable by anyone with visibility into that network path (connecting to the data-in-transit concept covered in a prior paper), while SSH provides the same remote-administration functionality but with the session genuinely encrypted end-to-end. Similarly, SNMPv1 (and v2c) use a weak, often-default, plaintext "community string" as its only form of authentication, offering essentially no real confidentiality or meaningful access control, while SNMPv3 adds genuine authentication and encryption to the same monitoring functionality. This "secure protocol selection" principle generalizes well beyond just these two examples (HTTPS over HTTP, SFTP/FTPS over plain FTP, and so on) — the standard remediation pattern is always the same: identify where a legacy, insecure protocol variant is still in use for a genuinely needed function, and replace it with its modern, secure equivalent providing the SAME functionality with meaningfully better protection, not accepting the insecure legacy option as an unavoidable limitation, and certainly not the reverse direction one distractor describes.');

VALUES ('c815f8bf-04fe-4e51-865a-a55ae677c599', '33e0acfc-a5b6-464a-9174-114fd493b942', 9, 'A company handling classified or highly sensitive information wants to prevent any possibility of electromagnetic signals emanating from equipment inside a specific room (which could theoretically be intercepted from outside to reconstruct data being processed) from leaking beyond that room''s walls, and also wants to block external wireless signals from reaching devices inside. What physical security measure specifically shields a space against electromagnetic signal leakage in both directions?');
VALUES ('c815f8bf-04fe-4e51-865a-a55ae677c599', '040dd6d4-be8b-4232-bb09-c9c61463e10e', 'A Faraday cage is specifically a conductive physical enclosure exploiting a real, well-understood electromagnetic principle — a sufficiently continuous conductive shell around a space blocks electromagnetic radiation from passing through it in either direction, which addresses two genuinely real and distinct security concerns simultaneously: preventing an attacker OUTSIDE the shielded space from potentially detecting/reconstructing sensitive data from unintentional electromagnetic emanations given off by electronic equipment inside (a real, if highly specialized, historical intelligence-collection technique), and preventing any external wireless signal from reaching (or any internal wireless signal from leaving) devices inside the shielded space at all, useful for environments requiring genuinely strict wireless isolation. This is a real, standard-enough physical security control for environments with severe confidentiality requirements, genuinely distinct from a mantrap (covered in a prior paper, which controls PHYSICAL PERSON entry via an interlocking-door vestibule, unrelated to electromagnetic shielding) and from a bastion host (covered in a prior paper, an entirely unrelated NETWORK architecture concept) — this is a demonstrated, real physical phenomenon and mitigation, not a technical impossibility.');

VALUES ('9fde2944-54e0-4669-8f3e-0742bf1da391', '33e0acfc-a5b6-464a-9174-114fd493b942', 10, 'A company wants to verify, at every system startup, that the boot process has not been tampered with — that only cryptographically signed, trusted firmware, bootloader, and OS components are allowed to execute in sequence, and that any unauthorized modification to any of these components (which might otherwise go completely undetected once the OS is running) would be caught before the system finishes booting. What security concept addresses this boot-time integrity verification?');
VALUES ('9fde2944-54e0-4669-8f3e-0742bf1da391', '96bdca8e-193c-4a00-aacd-e7af6308ea15', 'Secure boot specifically addresses a genuinely distinct and earlier point in a system''s lifecycle than runtime-focused controls — rather than monitoring for unauthorized CHANGES to files after the OS is already running (which is what File Integrity Monitoring, covered in a prior paper, specifically does, and which a sufficiently deep boot-level or rootkit-style compromise, covered earlier this paper, could potentially evade or precede entirely), secure/measured boot cryptographically verifies each component in the chain — firmware, bootloader, kernel — against a trusted signature BEFORE allowing it to execute, specifically catching tampering at the earliest possible moment, before the OS (and therefore before any OS-level security tool like FIM or EDR) is even running to observe anything. This directly addresses the specific threat of a "bootkit" (a rootkit-family compromise that targets the boot process itself, sometimes surviving even a full OS reinstall since it lives below the OS layer) — a genuinely more severe and harder-to-detect compromise than most runtime malware, precisely because it operates before any runtime security tooling has a chance to load at all. This is a real, standard, and increasingly widely-deployed hardware/firmware-level security capability (built into most modern systems), not a theoretical impossibility, and is a meaningfully earlier-stage, complementary control to FIM rather than a redundant restatement of it.');

-- ── Domain: Security Operations (28% — 6 questions) ──

VALUES ('e150e507-cfa7-47a4-ad46-78685bfb7904', '33e0acfc-a5b6-464a-9174-114fd493b942', 11, 'A development team wants to catch security vulnerabilities in their application''s own source code (like a hardcoded credential or an unsafe function call) BEFORE the code is even compiled or run, as an automated step in their CI/CD pipeline — as well as separately testing the RUNNING application itself for vulnerabilities like injection flaws that only manifest when the application is actually executing. What are these two complementary automated code-security testing approaches called?');
VALUES ('e150e507-cfa7-47a4-ad46-78685bfb7904', 'd753f355-5362-4553-9000-9f3cfae01d0f', 'SAST and DAST are genuinely complementary, distinct testing approaches operating at different points and from different vantage points: SAST examines the application''s actual SOURCE CODE (or in some cases compiled binaries) WITHOUT running it, meaning it can be integrated very early in a CI/CD pipeline (even before a build succeeds) and can directly flag issues like a hardcoded secret, an unsafe/deprecated function call, or a known-vulnerable coding pattern by directly inspecting the code text itself — but by definition, it can''t observe how the application actually BEHAVES when running, or catch vulnerabilities that only manifest from real runtime interaction. DAST instead tests the application from the OUTSIDE while it''s actually running (similar in spirit to how a vulnerability scanner or penetration tester, both covered in prior papers, would interact with a live target), which can catch genuinely different classes of issues — like an injection vulnerability, covered in a prior paper, that only manifests through actual runtime input handling — that source-code-only analysis might miss or that only emerge from the interaction of multiple components together. This is a real, standard, and increasingly automated part of a mature secure software development lifecycle, genuinely reducing (though not eliminating) the need for purely manual code review for every single change.');

VALUES ('584a21b4-930e-4496-8451-969714aeaef5', '33e0acfc-a5b6-464a-9174-114fd493b942', 12, 'A security analyst receives a suspicious email attachment and wants to observe exactly what it does — what files it creates, what network connections it attempts, what registry changes it makes — WITHOUT any risk of it actually infecting a real production system or spreading further if it turns out to be genuinely malicious. What technique/environment is purpose-built for this kind of safe, observed execution?');
VALUES ('584a21b4-930e-4496-8451-969714aeaef5', 'b01b290d-dc46-43fd-9c30-5ae73fb84453', 'Sandboxing is specifically the purpose-built technique for safely OBSERVING unknown or suspicious code''s actual behavior — the isolated, instrumented environment is deliberately designed so that even fully malicious behavior can be allowed to occur and be closely monitored (exactly what it tries to do, not just whether a static signature matches), while the isolation boundary itself prevents any of that observed behavior from actually reaching or affecting real production systems or being able to spread further, directly complementary to (and often feeding into) threat intelligence/IOC generation (covered in a prior paper) once a sandboxed sample''s malicious behavior and indicators are confirmed. This is a genuinely distinct concept from a honeynet (covered in a prior paper, a decoy NETWORK environment built to attract and study attacker BEHAVIOR from outside, rather than a controlled environment for directly executing one specific suspicious file under close observation) and from a bastion host (covered in a prior paper, an entirely unrelated network access-gateway concept) — sandboxing is a real, standard, and widely deployed technique specifically because directly running unknown code on a real system carries genuine, well-understood risk that isolated execution specifically mitigates.');

VALUES ('abfb11ed-3a6f-4bc3-87b0-521c8417f7cf', '33e0acfc-a5b6-464a-9174-114fd493b942', 13, 'A company wants to ensure that any device connecting to its internal corporate network — whether a company laptop, a visitor''s device, or an employee''s personal phone — is automatically checked against a security posture policy (up-to-date antivirus, current OS patches, disk encryption enabled) BEFORE being granted network access, and automatically quarantined onto a restricted network segment if it fails that check. What technology enforces this kind of pre-connection device posture check?');
VALUES ('abfb11ed-3a6f-4bc3-87b0-521c8417f7cf', '8876a29c-20ec-45a7-8b16-6d9e6c66564f', 'Network Access Control is specifically purpose-built for this pre-connection posture-verification gate — rather than a binary "on the network or not," NAC actively evaluates a connecting device against a defined, configurable security policy (checking things like patch currency, active/updated antivirus, disk encryption status) and can respond with a genuinely intermediate outcome: automatically routing a non-compliant device onto a restricted, quarantined network segment (often granting only enough access to reach a remediation resource, like a patch server) rather than the blunt extremes of either full trust or full denial. This directly connects to and extends the Zero Trust concept (covered in a prior paper, "never trust, always verify") into a concrete, automated, DEVICE-level enforcement mechanism specifically at the point of network connection, and complements micro-segmentation (covered in a prior paper, which limits lateral movement AFTER a device is already on the network) by controlling what happens at the moment of INITIAL connection instead. A write blocker (covered in a prior paper) is an entirely unrelated forensic evidence-preservation tool, and a honeytoken (covered in a prior paper) is a decoy tripwire — neither performs posture-based network access decisions; NAC is a real, standard, and widely deployed technology for exactly this need.');

VALUES ('8b9eff6d-f635-4efa-803c-7191d4edd08d', '33e0acfc-a5b6-464a-9174-114fd493b942', 14, 'Before a new application is even built, a security architect deliberately walks through the planned system''s architecture asking "what could go wrong here, and who would want to attack this component, and how" — systematically identifying potential attack paths, entry points, and threat actors relevant to the design, and using that analysis to influence the actual architecture before a single line of code is written. What is this proactive, design-phase security practice called?');
VALUES ('8b9eff6d-f635-4efa-803c-7191d4edd08d', 'b9d387cb-9a3d-4c27-b52b-ae59a3f6b399', 'Threat modeling is specifically a PROACTIVE, PRE-BUILD design-phase discipline — deliberately reasoning through a planned system''s architecture to identify realistic threats, likely attacker motivations/capabilities, and specific attack paths BEFORE implementation begins, so the resulting architecture can be adjusted to address identified risks from the start rather than being retrofitted with security controls after the fact; STRIDE is one common, structured methodology for categorizing the types of threats being considered during this process. This is a genuinely distinct activity from threat hunting (covered in a prior paper) despite the superficially similar name — threat hunting is REACTIVE and applied to an ALREADY-RUNNING, existing environment, proactively searching for evidence that a compromise may have already occurred and evaded existing detection, while threat modeling happens BEFORE a system even exists, with an entirely different goal (informing design decisions, not detecting an active compromise). A gap analysis (covered earlier this paper) compares a CURRENT state against a DESIRED target state generally, a different and broader planning activity than the specific "what could attack this planned architecture" analysis threat modeling performs — security absolutely IS incorporated at the design phase in mature development practices, contrary to the last distractor.');

VALUES ('b9299301-5594-40d8-a1b4-e84b3c9e817c', '33e0acfc-a5b6-464a-9174-114fd493b942', 15, 'A company runs a structured security exercise where one team (playing attackers) actively attempts to breach the organization''s defenses using real adversarial techniques, while a separate team (playing defenders) attempts to detect and respond to those attempts in real time, with a third, neutral team facilitating and evaluating both sides'' performance. What are these three roles called, and how does this differ from a standard, single-team penetration test?');
VALUES ('b9299301-5594-40d8-a1b4-e84b3c9e817c', '40c50dd8-0291-4716-83b0-c2f34fd5c2f5', 'The red team/blue team/purple team structure specifically extends beyond a standard penetration test (covered in a prior paper as vulnerability-scan-vs-pentest, which typically focuses on finding and demonstrating exploitable weaknesses, generally without also actively exercising the organization''s DEFENSIVE detection and response capability in real time) into a genuinely dual-sided exercise: the red team actively attacks using realistic adversarial techniques (echoing the same adversarial mindset as a pentest, but as an ongoing exercise rather than a single point-in-time assessment), the blue team is the organization''s actual defensive function (SOC analysts, using tools like SIEM and EDR, both covered in prior papers) attempting to detect and respond to the red team''s activity in real time, and purple team specifically denotes a coordinating function ensuring both sides actively share findings and lessons DURING (not just after) the exercise, maximizing the value extracted for improving actual defensive capability rather than treating the exercise purely as an adversarial "gotcha." This is a genuinely real, standard, and increasingly common security exercise structure specifically because it tests something a standalone penetration test doesn''t directly test: the defenders'' own real-world detection and response performance under realistic pressure, not merely whether vulnerabilities technically exist.');

VALUES ('34948f1f-d28e-4fb7-8458-59a50790784c', '33e0acfc-a5b6-464a-9174-114fd493b942', 16, 'A company''s Business Continuity Plan defines an alternate facility that already has fully configured, ready-to-use hardware and software with near-real-time data replication from the primary site, allowing operations to resume there within minutes to a few hours of a primary-site disaster — at significantly higher ongoing cost than a facility with only basic infrastructure (power, connectivity, physical space) but no pre-installed systems, which would take days or weeks to become operational. What are these two types of alternate processing sites called?');
VALUES ('34948f1f-d28e-4fb7-8458-59a50790784c', '24223e7d-3674-4894-b8d2-b75af4ce0494', 'Hot, warm, and cold sites form a standard spectrum of alternate-facility readiness directly analogous to (and part of the same overall planning discipline as) BCP/DR (covered in a prior paper) and BIA/RTO/RPO (also covered in a prior paper) — a hot site is essentially fully ready to go, with near-real-time data replication and pre-installed, configured systems, enabling the fastest possible recovery time at the HIGHEST ongoing cost (since all that readiness has to be maintained continuously, whether or not a disaster ever actually occurs); a cold site provides only basic infrastructure (power, connectivity, physical space) with no pre-installed systems or current data, at the LOWEST ongoing cost but requiring meaningfully more time (days to weeks) to actually become operational after a disaster is declared; and a warm site sits between the two on both dimensions — some infrastructure and periodically-updated (not necessarily real-time) data already in place. Choosing among these three tiers is a direct RTO/RPO-versus-cost trade-off decision, the same underlying trade-off pattern that appears in the DR strategy tiers (backup/restore through pilot light through warm standby through multi-site active/active) covered generally in AWS SAA content, applied here as the general, cloud-agnostic Security+ BCP concept — this is a real, standard, and directly testable classification, not an arbitrary or non-existent distinction.');

-- ── Domain: Security Program Management & Oversight (20% — 4 questions) ──

VALUES ('e9398de4-459c-4856-a6eb-8c364157f4fc', '33e0acfc-a5b6-464a-9174-114fd493b942', 17, 'A company hiring for a position with access to highly sensitive financial systems requires, BEFORE the new hire''s first day, a criminal background check, verification of claimed prior employment and education, and a signed non-disclosure agreement acknowledging the confidential nature of the data they''ll access. What is this pre-employment security process called, and how does it relate to offboarding?');
VALUES ('e9398de4-459c-4856-a6eb-8c364157f4fc', 'f26e1034-2c7e-4f47-8020-078c98756e93', 'Security-focused onboarding is the direct, symmetric counterpart to offboarding (covered in a prior paper, which specifically addresses TIMELY access REVOCATION when employment ends) — establishing a baseline of verified trust and legal accountability BEFORE any sensitive access is ever granted in the first place: a criminal background check helps assess risk before extending trust, employment/education verification confirms the candidate''s claimed history is actually accurate (catching outright fabrication before it becomes a problem), and a signed NDA creates real legal accountability and consequence specifically for any future unauthorized disclosure of confidential information the employee will be exposed to. Together, thorough onboarding vetting and prompt offboarding de-provisioning bracket the FULL lifecycle of an employee''s relationship with sensitive systems — a genuinely real, standard, and directly complementary pair of practices (not the same single practice under two names, and not one substituting for the other), and pre-employment vetting is a well-documented, real risk-reduction practice specifically because a bad hire with access to sensitive systems is a genuine, material organizational risk.');

VALUES ('3310891e-a2ec-4273-92eb-788c7c372946', '33e0acfc-a5b6-464a-9174-114fd493b942', 18, 'A company must store certain categories of customer data — specifically for customers located in a particular country — physically within that same country''s borders, per that country''s specific legal requirement, regardless of where the company itself is headquartered or where its other infrastructure is located. What is this specific legal/regulatory requirement called, and how does it differ from GDPR''s extraterritorial reach?');
VALUES ('3310891e-a2ec-4273-92eb-788c7c372946', 'b0a66171-f9e6-4e41-a40c-44be120a351d', 'Data sovereignty (also called data localization or data residency) addresses a genuinely distinct legal DIMENSION from GDPR''s extraterritorial reach (covered in a prior paper) — GDPR is fundamentally about WHOSE data is protected (EU residents'' personal data) and applies to any organization processing it REGARDLESS of where that organization or its infrastructure is physically located, while data sovereignty is specifically about WHERE data must be physically stored (and in some cases processed), driven by a specific country''s own legal requirement that certain categories of data about its residents, or data generated within its borders, must remain within that country''s physical jurisdiction, independent of who the processing organization is or where it''s headquartered. A company could, in principle, face BOTH requirements simultaneously and separately for the same dataset — needing to protect EU residents'' data per GDPR''s rules (a WHO-focused requirement) while ALSO needing to physically store some of that same data within a specific country''s borders per that country''s own separate data-sovereignty law (a WHERE-focused requirement) — these are genuinely complementary, not identical or mutually exclusive, legal dimensions, and this is a real, well-documented, and increasingly significant category of international compliance requirement directly relevant to any organization operating cloud infrastructure across multiple countries/regions.');

VALUES ('7f039762-5b00-4a92-b9ea-0e79d59b7f45', '33e0acfc-a5b6-464a-9174-114fd493b942', 19, 'A company negotiating with a critical software vendor wants a formal, contractually-binding commitment guaranteeing 99.9% uptime with specific financial penalties if that uptime isn''t met, while separately drafting a non-binding document with a different business partner simply documenting a shared general understanding of an informal collaboration. What are these two genuinely different types of inter-organizational agreements called?');
VALUES ('7f039762-5b00-4a92-b9ea-0e79d59b7f45', '3a95801b-4221-4bf9-a814-ee5b28e88cec', 'These three agreement types sit at genuinely different points on a spectrum of formality and specificity, and choosing the RIGHT one for a given relationship is a real, practical governance decision: an SLA is specifically about MEASURABLE PERFORMANCE commitments (uptime percentage, response time, specific remediation timelines) with real, typically financial, consequences for non-compliance — the appropriate instrument when a company needs actual contractual leverage and accountability from a vendor or partner, directly connecting to vendor risk management (covered in a prior paper) as one of the tools used to formalize and enforce vendor expectations. An MOU, by contrast, is deliberately LESS formal and generally NOT legally binding in the same enforceable way — it documents a shared general understanding or intent between parties, useful for establishing a working relationship''s broad shape without the weight of a fully negotiated, penalty-bearing contract. A BPA formalizes the specific terms of an actual ongoing business PARTNERSHIP (including things like profit-sharing or liability allocation between the partnering organizations) — a genuinely different purpose from either an SLA''s performance-guarantee focus or an MOU''s general-understanding focus. These are real, standard, and distinct agreement types organizations genuinely choose between based on how much binding formality and enforceability a given relationship actually requires, not interchangeable labels for one single kind of document.');

VALUES ('f45e6f54-702f-4387-8e56-25097ca23da7', '33e0acfc-a5b6-464a-9174-114fd493b942', 20, 'A company building a new customer-facing application decides, from the very beginning of the design process, to collect only the minimum personal data genuinely necessary for the application to function, to encrypt that data by default, and to build user-facing privacy controls into the initial release — rather than treating privacy as something to be added on later once the application is already built and a compliance requirement forces the issue. What principle does this proactive, built-in-from-the-start approach represent?');
VALUES ('f45e6f54-702f-4387-8e56-25097ca23da7', 'bec9e47b-a4da-4dd9-b929-26707c6fb6d4', 'Privacy by design is specifically a PROACTIVE, foundational principle — treating privacy protection as a core design requirement from the very start of a system''s architecture (data minimization: collecting only what''s genuinely necessary; encryption by default; building genuine user-facing privacy controls into the initial release) rather than as a compliance checkbox bolted on reactively after the system already exists, directly complementary to (but distinct from) the Data Protection Officer role (covered in a prior paper, an ONGOING GOVERNANCE ROLE overseeing compliance, rather than a specific DESIGN-PHASE architectural principle) and to threat modeling (covered earlier this paper, a similarly proactive, design-phase security practice — privacy by design applies that same "build it in from the start" philosophy specifically to privacy rather than general security threats). This is a real, increasingly formalized principle that several modern privacy regulations explicitly reference or effectively require, directly connecting to and reinforcing the general theme running through this entire paper''s Program Management questions: mature security and privacy governance is fundamentally about PROACTIVE, BUILT-IN practices (gap analysis, onboarding vetting, threat modeling, privacy by design) rather than purely reactive, after-the-fact remediation.');
